        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Ah, sorry, I see now that this is default behavior, also without mini.comment. (I was just used to different behavior from vim.) Would be nice to be able to have the commenting behavior be more consistent (i.e., still not ignoring empty lines when there are no non-empty ones; having a no-op keybinding isn't all that useful, IMO).\n\nIf by \"default\" you mean coming from built-in Neovim's `gc` and `gcc`, then yes the behavior is the same. Mostly because the built-in one was heavily modeled by 'mini.comment'.\n\nNot adding comment to only blank lines is [a documented behavior](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8ca3965e68edfee5f438d4063f5adf5afa255884/doc/mini-comment.txt#L180-L183): \"It uncomments if lines are comment (every line is a comment or blank) ...\". The region with only blank lines is considered to be comment and the action is to uncomment (i.e. make it not comment), which in this case is the same as leaving them as is.\n\nAs I consider the action \"comment blank lines\" to be relatively niche, this looks acceptable to me. A better solution might be to [add an Insert mode mapping to insert comment leaders at cursor](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1837#issuecomment-2934018027), but that is hard to do properly out of the box with 'mini.comment' (adding new mapping might break user's setups plus there is no good left hand side for such Insert mode mapping).",
