        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Sorry for implying that the current behavior was inconsistent – that is certainly just a matter of perspective. It is certainly consistent with uncommenting when there's a mixture of comments and blanks. It's just not consistent with commenting when there's a mixture of non-comments and blanks – or toggling between the two?\n\nAt least I would have thought that the consistent thing to do would be to still toggle even if there is just a single blank line, just like one does with a single commented non-blank. I.e., if there is text on a line, `gcc` will add and remove the comment alternatively, but if there's a commented-out empty line, it will just remove the comment character and then become a no-op.\n\nI understand the issue with the indent. The behavior I had in vim, and that seems to be the default in other plugins (though I haven't researched that thoroughly) is to just use an indent of 0, but … yeah. This is certainly an edge-case, and I see that your design here makes perfect sense. I just need a quick shortcut to comment out empty lines, but I'll manage that in some other way. Thanks.",
